:0.50189,"y":0.36006,"w":0.01781,"h":0.01432,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"My"},{"x":0.50398,"y":0.37078,"w":0.0328,"h":0.02034,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"M ="},{"x":0.73218,"y":0.37078,"w":0.0328,"h":0.02034,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"M ="},{"x":0.47023,"y":0.37079,"w":0.02424,"h":0.01346,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"x ="},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.393,"w":0.2412,"h":0.02323,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"The centroid is the point (1>2, 2>5)."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.44017,"w":0.14552,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Moments of Inertia"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.46483,"w":0.5931,"h":0.07418,"fs":0.01279,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"An object’s first moments (Table 15.1) tell us about balance and about the torque the object experiences about different axes in a gravitational field. If the object is a rotating shaft, however, we are more likely to be interested in how much energy is stored in the shaft or about how much energy is generated by a shaft rotating at a particular angular velocity. This is where the second moment or moment of inertia comes in."},{"x":0.17473,"y":0.5158,"w":0.04623,"h":0.02206,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"Δmk rku"},{"x":0.1626,"y":0.52808,"w":0.0113,"h":0.01268,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"yk"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.54088,"w":0.59301,"h":0.05951,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"Think of partitioning the shaft into small blocks of mass ∆mk and let rk denote the distance from the kth block’s center of mass to the axis of rotation (Figure 15.38). If the shaft rotates at a constant angular velocity of v = du>dt radians per second, the block’s center of mass will trace its orbit at a linear speed of"},{"x":0.17569,"y":0.54579,"w":0.0088,"h":0.01214,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"rk"},{"x":0.23634,"y":0.56734,"w":0.08104,"h":0.0357,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"Axis of rotation"},{"x":0.60207,"y":0.60522,"w":0.09829,"h":0.02804,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dt (rku) = rkdu"},{"x":0.56435,"y":0.60597,"w":0.04815,"h":0.02188,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"yk = d"},{"x":0.68601,"y":0.6125,"w":0.06373,"h":0.02074,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dt = rkv."},{"x":0.0886,"y":0.62983,"w":0.25888,"h":0.10434,"fs":0.01151,"n":8,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 15.38 To find an integral for the amount of energy stored in a rotating shaft, we first imagine the shaft to be parti- tioned into small blocks. Each block has its own kinetic energy. We add the contribu- tions of the individual blocks to find the kinetic energy of the shaft."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.63876,"w":0.32139,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"The block’s kinetic energy will be approximately"},{"x":0.52397,"y":0.6573,"w":0.26624,"h":0.02703,"fs":0.01279,"n":6,"k":0,"t":"1 2 ∆mkyk 2 = 1 2 ∆mk(rkv)2 = 1 2v2rk 2 ∆mk."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.68992,"w":0.34773,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"The kinetic energy of the shaft will be approximately"},{"x":0.62844,"y":0.70846,"w":0.08169,"h":0.02703,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"1 2v2rk 2 ∆mk."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.74107,"w":0.59295,"h":0.02814,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"The integral approached by these sums as the shaft is partitioned into smaller and smaller blocks gives the shaft’s kinetic energy:"},{"x":0.60811,"y":0.78008,"w":0.13195,"h":0.03065,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":0,"t":"1 2v2r2 dm = 1 2v2 L"},{"x":0.74384,"y":0.78262,"w":0.20767,"h":0.01728,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"r2 dm. (1)"},{"x":0.52,"y":0.78643,"w":0.06094,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"KEshaft ="},{"x":0.36259,"y":0.81269,"w":0.06751,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"The factor"},{"x":0.66345,"y":0.83378,"w":0.03674,"h":0.01727,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"r2 dm"},{"x":0.61389,"y":0.83759,"w":0.02246,"h":0.01346,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"I ="},{"x":0.36259,"y":0.86895,"w":0.59304,"h":0.02815,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"is the moment of inertia of the shaft about its axis of rotation, and we see from Equation (1) that the shaft’s kinetic energy is"},{"x":0.60494,"y":0.90797,"w":0.07653,"h":0.0217,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"KEshaft = 1"},{